From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com>
Date: 02 Nov 94 03:24:50 EST
Subject: [#94-11-26] Argument by Analogy (Uploads)

Craig Presson wrote 11/1/94: <...the toaster cannot make and defend
claims or enter into and perform contracts.>

Toasters, that is, machines, can't do this, ok.  

<"Free will" does not distinguish the class of machines from the class
of humans, since I can demonstrate simple non-deterministic machines, and
even, in the computer, evolved machines...>

This is a philosophical statement.  Since you maintain that philosophy is
useless and boring because it doesn't "do" anything in the way of "direct
action," this statement must have been useless and boring for you to type.  I
know you don't want to engage in a philosophical discussion about machine
classes vs. human classes, let alone about "free will" because it would lead to
an inexhaustible string of linked concepts, and much time-consuming back and
forth before we could begin to clear up what this statement even says.  Let's
not do it.  As you say, the people with the will and stomach for it are working
on it, happily.  Maybe the results of their work will be apparent soon, maybe
not so soon, maybe never.

<It is Ph 101 in the sense that it is a very fundamental question that
philosophy does not have the tools to handle, which is why you see it as a
grown-up question. Philosophy as an enterprise is in shambles. Philosophers
can't even agree on a philosophy of science, even while scientists have achieved
rings around them for 374 years.>

What tools, besides a brain, does philosophy need?  Philosophy as an enterprise
is in shambles?  Perhaps that's the reason that so much of Science as an
enterprise is in shambles.  Particle physics, cosmology, and the social sciences
in particular and many other branches of specialized knowledge to a lesser
degree (any science that deals with messy interferences by humanity no matter
how indirectly).  Why is it surprising that philosophers don't agree?  I haven't
noticed that scientists are a particularly agreeable bunch of 'learned
ignoramuses' either, in fact, when they start agreeing, usually they are
marching off a dogmatic cliff somewhere.  As far as scientists achieving rings
around them, I would say that philosophy has launched and propped up the whole
scientific enterprise from day one till now.

<Sorry, I will not be treated like a child by philosophers or anyone else. We've
outgrown it -- George Boole delivered the coup de grace 100 years ago, by
mathematizing the last useful bit of philosophy.>

Mathematics is a pretty thing, but it is a teensy weensy subset of all human
knowledge, which is itself a teensy weensy subset of everything in the universe,
which is the subject matter of philosophy.

<Show me one thing a philosopher has accomplished in his subject in the last 50
years.>

It's true that philosophical concepts don't launch themselves onto "Oprah" and
"Inside Edition" with great speed, often it takes centuries or millenia to
really sink into a society.  For example, our friend Rousseau's ideas have
pretty much debilitated Western Civilization and its previously unwavering
support for science, technology and capitalism, mostly within the last thirty
years.  The modern welfare state and the leftist attack on rationality, progress
and excellence in general are all the result of philosophic accomplishments.  We
have been losing ground quickly, and scientific concepts aren't going to get us
out of it, especially since the educational system is dumbing-down it's third
generation in a row.  Now it's infusing them with Rousseauean neo-pagan
environmentalist irrationality and the Hegelian/Marxist view of life as
elaborate collective machines, anti-individualistic in the extreme.  Only
philosophic concepts can reverse this alarming trend, like the Extropian
Principles, as an example.

